WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
He Was Angry at His Phone Company. Then, a 'Tipping Point'

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:19.000
Customer service hotlines exist in part so that customers can air grievances, and one elderly man in Japan apparently took advantage of that.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:31.000
CNN reports 71-year-old Akitoshi Okamoto was arrested last November on suspicion of fraudulent interference of business.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:42.000
He allegedly made 24,000 phone calls over a two-and-a-half-year period to a toll-free KDDI number.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:51.000
Mr. Okamoto claims that the telecommunications company violated its service contract with him.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:59.000
KDDI says it started getting the calls from Okamoto in May 2017.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:12.000
By October of 2019, the company had had enough and put in a damage report with police, saying Okamoto's calls "seriously interfered with its business,"

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.000
keeping operators from taking care of other job duties and customers.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:27.000
According to Metropolitan Police in Tokyo, Okamoto would call KDDI and either harangue and insult whomever took his call,

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:36.000
insist that someone from customer service pay him a personal visit, or simply call and then hang up.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:52.000
At first, the company didn't want to get the police involved, but that the "tipping point" came when Okamoto called KDDI 411 times in one week in October 2019.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:59.000
Per Japanese media, Okamoto is claiming he's the victim, not KDDI. 

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.000
His beef with KDDI is that the handset he bought from them can't pick up radio broadcasts. 

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:23.000
It should be noted that as Japan's older population grows, there's been an uptick in the "social problems" involving the elderly, including car accidents and an increase in violence on public transportation.
